  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nCurrent conditions/this morning:\nHigh pressure is slowly sliding east this morning, with light \nand variable winds. Satellite imagery is showing some high level\nclouds pushing into the area from the west, but we will still \nhave good radiational cooling conditions, and while I do not \nexpect us to get quite as cold as last night, it will still be \ncool with lows in the 40s and perhaps upper 30s. Conditions will\nalso be good for fog, bringing some patchy dense fog that will \nburn off quickly after sunrise. \n\nToday: \nSoutherly warm air advection will push highs up into the mid \n70s to around 80 across the area. We have been watching the \npotential for showers and thunderstorms approaching the area \nwith a wave of warm air advection early this morning, which we \ncan see have developed over northern North Dakota as of 1 AM. \nHowever, most mesoscale analysis does not support this \nconvection getting much farther east than it already is, and \nshould deteriorate to mainly cloud debris before it gets to the \nNorthland. Precipitation chances increase tonight as the main \nupper level shortwave moves across the area Tuesday into \nWednesday. Tonight there will be enough instability and decent \ndeep layer shear to initiate convection over eastern North \nDakota, then gradually slide east overnight. However, like \ntonight, the instability and shear weakens significantly to the \neast, and the storms are likely to weaken and dissipate as they \nmove east. We do have a sliver of Marginal severe risk just to \nour west from SPC, but it and the even farther west Slight risk \nare likely to be the main concern of the night and am less \nconcerned for the Northland. \n\nTuesday: Warmer and more humid conditions push into the area, \nwith surface based instability developing during the afternoon \nand evening hours. Then, just as the upper level shortwave moves\nacross the area and deep layer shear parameters increase, there\nwill be another wave of showers and thunderstorms which move \nacross the Northland. These storms will have the potential to be\nstrong to severe storms, with a few storms capable of producing\n1\" hail and damaging winds. SPC has a marginal risk (level 1 \nout of 5) outlined for all of the Northland. \n\nShowers and non-severe storms will redevelop across the area on\nWednesday in the cyclonic flow behind the upper level \nshortwave. While we will have mostly cyclonic flow on Thursday, \nthe surface ridge building into the area and low level dry air \nshould keep anything but diurnal cumulus from forming. Wednesday\nand Thursday will be cooler again as well. \n\nDry and seasonable conditions to continue into Friday with high\npressure lingering over the area. Weak upper level ridging \nbuilds into the area for the weekend, with poor predictability \nfor precipitation chances as weak shortwaves may slide through \nthe flow aloft and bring a round of showers/storms. The forecast\nhas some low end chances for precipitation, but these may be \nmore limited than is currently depicted. Temperatures should \nremain near normal through the weekend. Another potent upper \nlevel low will progress across the area early next week, which \nmay bring another round of higher confidence precipitation \nchances.",
